; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Rhyme Finder
; CATEGORY: Words & Text
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     rhymes, words, recursion, butlast, poems
; SUMMARY:  Finds words that rhyme by comparing their endings from the last vowel sound, then writes silly two-line rhymes.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Two words usually rhyme when they end the same way from their last
;   vowel onwards: c-AT and h-AT, n-IGHT and k-NIGHT, b-OAT and c-OAT.
;   The program cuts off that "rhyme ending", finds every word in its
;   list with the same ending, and uses them to write little poems.
;   English spelling is famously odd, so it is fooled sometimes:
;   "though" and "tough" look alike but do not sound alike.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run. Type a word to find its rhymes; quit or Enter stops.
;   You can also type   print rhymeending "starlight
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * working from the END of a word with LAST and BUTLAST
;   * a procedure with two phases: skip consonants, then collect vowels
;   * FILTER to gather matching words, REMOVE to drop the word itself
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Add more words to the list so more rhymes are found
;   * Treat y at the end of a word as a vowel (happy, snappy)
;   * Fix a spelling trap: make "ough" words never rhyme with each other
; ============================================================

make "rhymebank [cat hat bat mat sat flat chat that rat night light kite ~
  bright fight knight might sight white bite boat coat goat float note ~
  wrote tree free see bee three me knee sea tea fun sun run bun one ~
  done ton day play say may away grey stay way hop top stop shop drop ~
  mop cake bake lake make snake take shake rain train brain chain plane ~
  dog frog log fog jog bog star car far jar bar guitar moon soon spoon ~
  june tune balloon though tough]

to vowelp :ch
  output memberp :ch "aeiou
end

; Walk back from the end of the word. First keep consonants (the tail
; after the last vowel), then keep the vowels in front of them, and
; stop at the first consonant before those vowels.
; A silent e (snake, tune) does not make its own sound, so we find the
; ending of the word without it and put the e back: snak -> ak -> ake.
to rhymeending :wd
  if (count :wd) < 2 [output :wd]
  if and equalp last :wd "e not vowelp last butlast :wd ~
    [output word rhymeending butlast :wd "e]
  output vowelpart butlast :wd last :wd
end

; Phase one: still collecting the consonants at the end.
to vowelpart :rest :ending
  if emptyp :rest [output :ending]
  if vowelp first :ending [output vowelrun :rest :ending]
  output vowelpart butlast :rest word last :rest :ending
end

; Phase two: we have reached the vowels; take every vowel in a row.
to vowelrun :rest :ending
  if emptyp :rest [output :ending]
  if not vowelp last :rest [output :ending]
  output vowelrun butlast :rest word last :rest :ending
end

to rhymesof :wd
  localmake "end rhymeending lowercase :wd
  output remove lowercase :wd filter [equalp rhymeending ? :end] :rhymebank
end

to showrhymes :wd
  localmake "found rhymesof :wd
  ifelse emptyp :found ~
    [print (sentence [Nothing in my list rhymes with] :wd)] ~
    [print (sentence :wd [rhymes with] :found)]
end

; A two line poem: two random rhyming words dropped into fill-in lines.
to couplet :wd
  localmake "found rhymesof :wd
  if emptyp :found [stop]
  localmake "one pick :found
  ; pick the second word from the others so it differs from the first
  localmake "others remove :one :found
  localmake "two ifelse emptyp :others [:one] [pick :others]
  print (sentence [I once saw a] :wd [beside a big] :one)
  print (sentence [and it smiled at me, then it ate a] :two)
  print []
end

to askloop
  print [Type a word to rhyme, or quit:]
  localmake "line readlist
  if or emptyp :line equalp :line [quit] [stop]
  showrhymes first :line
  askloop
end

foreach [cat night boat tree moon] [showrhymes ?]
print []
print [Two silly poems:]
couplet "goat
couplet "snake
askloop
